-- book.sh --
#!/bin/sh
# pin28-helper-package-entrypoints-discarded (`28M` §7/§8, the COMMISSIONED package shape) — half
# TWO of two, and the half that decides whether the packaging is worth anything.
#
# Half one proved the package's own entrypoints lift with their cross-file helper closure intact.
# This one proves the OTHER direction the human asked for: that an admin can discard or swap the
# entrypoints file and keep the helpers' function. If that fails, the split is decoration — the
# thin file is only worth being thin if replacing it is cheap.
#
# The spelling here is the bluntest of the three the design offers (`unset -f` per member, not
# sourcing it, or supplying your own): the entrypoints file is simply NOT LOADED. Only
# `wombat-helpers.oracle.sh` is on the command line, and this book supplies its own
# `wombat__is_converged` over that package's helpers — the replacement is not even an oracle file,
# just a function in a runbook.
#
# THAT IS THE SPELLING THIS CELL PINS AS DE-LICENSED. The book's entrypoint is a first-class
# definition source (`28K` §2a: an in-book role function is an ordinary oracle, recognized by name
# alone), but a BOOK mints no speaker — not by co-loading, and not by sourcing either
# (`rul-speaker-minting-is-oracle-sourcing-only`). So its reach into a DIFFERENT author's helpers
# file leaves its own custody and the vouch suspends: the site runs, attributed, and the mocks
# record the book's own `wombat sync`.
#
# The commissioned property survives in the spelling the ruling leaves: an admin who wants their own
# entrypoints writes them in a file that SOURCES the helpers, which takes custody. Wanting somebody
# else's helpers under your own vouch is exactly the act of authoring an oracle.
#
# The no-contest property is unaffected and still worth the cell: the helpers carry no `__` names,
# so nothing the package ships can collide with what the admin wrote.
#
# Note the definition sits ABOVE its site, which the full-positional regime requires
# (`28K` §2 rul-visibility-is-full-positional): a definition below licenses nothing above it.
wombat__is_converged() {
   _wombat_same "$2"
}

hork provision
wombat sync a.conf

-- wombat-helpers.oracle.sh --
# dorc-lang/v0.2
# THE SAME HELPERS FILE as half one, byte-for-byte in substance: the bulk of the package under
# non-role names. The admin kept this and threw the entrypoints away.
WOMBAT_ROOT=/etc/wombat

_wombat_dest() {
   printf '%s\n' "$WOMBAT_ROOT/$1"
}

_wombat_same() {
   wombat cmp -- "$1" "$(_wombat_dest "$1")"
}

-- probe-results.txt --
# No records: the suspended vouch ships no check, so the probe has nothing to ask and the site runs.

-- mocks/.log --
# newline-safe argv logger for the inert mock shims (slice-4). Sourced by each shim via
# `. "${0%/*}/.log"` (the shim's own dir); a DOTFILE so gate-5/gate-6's `ls`-derived shimset
# never lists it. Shims run under PATH=mocks-only ⇒ BUILTINS ONLY (no sed/awk).
_dorc_logged() {
  _dl_out="ran: ${0##*/}"
  for _dl_a in "$@"; do
    _dl_o=""                                          # pass 1: \ -> \\
    while case $_dl_a in *\\*) true ;; *) false ;; esac; do
      _dl_o="$_dl_o${_dl_a%%\\*}\\\\"; _dl_a=${_dl_a#*\\}
    done
    _dl_a="$_dl_o$_dl_a"
    _dl_o=""                                          # pass 2: newline -> \n
    while case $_dl_a in *"$_DL_NL"*) true ;; *) false ;; esac; do
      _dl_o="$_dl_o${_dl_a%%"$_DL_NL"*}\\n"; _dl_a=${_dl_a#*"$_DL_NL"}
    done
    _dl_out="$_dl_out $_dl_o$_dl_a"
  done
  printf '%s\n' "$_dl_out" >>"$DORC_LOG"
}
_DL_NL='
'

-- mocks/hork --
#!/bin/sh
# INERT mock for the unmodeled wall command.
. "${0%/*}/.log"; _dorc_logged "$@"
exit 0

-- mocks/wombat --
#!/bin/sh
# INERT mock. Reached through the discarded-entrypoint path: the BOOK's own role function into the
# package's helpers. An unresolved cross-author helper makes this rc 127 and the run-log stays empty.
. "${0%/*}/.log"; _dorc_logged "$@"
exit 0

-- expected.ran --
ran: hork provision
ran: wombat sync a.conf

-- replay --
$ dorc --book=book.sh --pre-source wombat-helpers.oracle.sh --results - < probe-results.txt
reserved: warning[reserved-namespace-squat]: Book function
`wombat__is_converged` sits in the `__is_converged` namespace reserved for
oracle functions; Dorc treats it as ordinary shell and runs it verbatim, but a
shipped oracle preamble defining the same name would collide with it. Rename it
to stay clear of `*__is_converged`.
      [ book.sh:32:1
   32 | wombat__is_converged() {
      | \__________________/
vouching: warning[vouched-composition-not-present]: [unwritten:
vouched-composition-not-present]
      [ book.sh:32:1
   32 | wombat__is_converged() {
      | \__________________/
#!/bin/sh
# dorc probe (read-only): checks per-SITE convergence, mutates nothing.
# When run, emits one results-record per site on stdout (the return channel):
#   site <leafid> effect=<holds|absent|cant-tell> rc=<n>
# effect is derived from the probe command's rc (0=holds, 1=absent, else cant-tell);
# rc is the raw PROBE-command status (opaque to Dorc -- the record is the out-of-band lane).

printf 'dorc-records/1 nonce=dorc attempt=1 host=localhost book=e45dfc5c5a623427047aff2a42411aeb7f64f8a4712ba1121f79667a0d715187 sites=0 @@dorc@@\n'
# site:0 unresolvable-no-probe
# site:1 unresolvable-no-probe
printf 'dorc-records-end/1 nonce=dorc @@dorc@@\n'
classify: note[cmdsub-operand-top]: operand 1 is an unresolvable positional
parameter (`$@`/`$*`/`$N`), so Dorc cannot know its value until the command runs
on the host -- there is nothing to resolve and no read-only probe to check. Dorc
elides a command only when it can prove the command's effect is already in
place, so this one is left to run on every apply.
      [ book.sh:33:17
   33 |    _wombat_same "$2"
      |                 \__/

   = repair:  Give the operand a value Dorc can resolve statically -- a literal,
              or a variable assigned from one -- so Dorc can probe it and elide
              the command once it has converged. If the value must stay dynamic,
              load an oracle that vouches for this command's convergence, and
              Dorc will guard the command instead of running it every time.
probe: note[site-unresolvable]: sm 2 sites run unprobed (no read-only check
could be shipped): `hork provision`, `wombat sync a.conf` -- run `dorc why` for
the per-site detail (the apply runs each anyway, to stay safe)
      [ book.sh:36:1
   36 | hork provision
      | \____________/

   = help:  sm site runs `hork provision`
why: ran because operand 1 is a command-substitution `$(...)` or runtime-dynamic value -- its value couldn't be resolved (first seen at 33:4 `_wombat_same "$2"`); so dorc runs it, to stay safe (when unsure, run). to skip it, make the operand a literal Dorc can resolve+probe [resolve-dynamism]
hint: 'hork' (line 36) is unmodeled: it is the first wall -- an oracle vouching its convergence would elide it when converged; 1 more unmodeled wall -- dorc why
dorc: run `dorc why` for the per-site cause-chains, or `dorc why book.sh:N` to query a source line
#!/bin/sh
# dorc apply: the book, with already-converged/dead lines elided (value-preserving stand-in).

